// ===========================================================================
// 49-aquarius-udisks.rules — mount any drive, inside or plugged in, with no
// password, for the person sitting at this computer
// ===========================================================================
// PLAIN ENGLISH
//
// polkit is the part of Linux that decides "is this person allowed to do this,
// and do they need to type a password first?". This file is one rule: the
// person actually sitting at this computer may mount, unmount and eject a
// drive — a USB stick, an SD card, an external SSD, AND (since 2026-10-19) a
// drive that lives inside the computer — without a password, exactly like on
// a Mac.
//
// It is what makes these work with no prompt:
//   * every drive inside the computer opening by itself when you log in, and
//     every drive you plug in opening the moment you plug it in
//     (/usr/libexec/aquarius-automount asks udisks2 to do both), and
//   * the dock's and Files' right-click "Eject / Unmount".
//
// It is loaded before Fedora's own udisks rules because its filename sorts
// before "50-" — so for the actions below, our answer is the one that stands.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// ⚠️ WHAT CHANGED ON 2026-10-19, AND WHY
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// udisks2 uses a DIFFERENT polkit action depending on the kind of drive:
//
//     filesystem-mount          a drive you PLUG IN
//     filesystem-mount-system   a drive INSIDE the computer
//
// Until 2026-10-19 this rule answered only the first one, on purpose: an
// inside drive asked for an administrator password, and the build STOPPED if
// filesystem-mount-system ever appeared here. A feature called "ask once"
// (FEATURES 020) softened that by writing chosen inside drives into
// /etc/fstab.
//
// Royce reversed that on 2026-10-19:
//
//     "Remove verification for all internal and external drives. Just mount
//      them from login and show in dock."
//
// So filesystem-mount-system is now granted too, the "ask once" feature is
// retired, and the build gate was reversed deliberately — it now checks that
// this rule DOES grant it, and that the session agent's never-touch list (the
// thing that actually keeps the machine's own partitions safe) still holds.
// The decision record: ../docs/decision-2026-10-19-gnome-only-and-open-drives.md
// (in the AquariusOS project folder, one level above this repository).
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// WHY THIS IS STILL SAFE — AND WHAT IT DELIBERATELY DOES NOT DO
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 1. WHO. `subject.local && subject.active` means "the login session that owns
//    the screen and keyboard right now". Not someone connected over SSH (not
//    local), not a session that has been switched away from (not active), and
//    never the login screen. A password-free mount is only ever granted to the
//    person physically here. This line did NOT change, and the build checks it.
//
// 2. THE MACHINE'S OWN DISK IS ALREADY MOUNTED. The partitions AquariusOS runs
//    from are mounted by the system at start-up, before anybody logs in, and a
//    mounted partition cannot be mounted again. The EFI partition, Windows'
//    recovery partition and the rest of the start-up plumbing are left alone by
//    the agent's never-touch list (see the header of
//    /usr/libexec/aquarius-automount), and udisks2 hides most of them anyway.
//
// 3. ENCRYPTED STAYS ENCRYPTED. Unlocking an encrypted drive is a different
//    action (encrypted-unlock), not granted here. A LUKS or BitLocker drive
//    still needs its own passphrase — the only "verification" left, because
//    without the key there is nothing to read.
//
// It also does NOT grant:
//   * filesystem-mount-other-seat — a drive attached to ANOTHER seat (another
//     screen-and-keyboard on a multi-seat machine). Not something this
//     computer has, so not something to hand out.
//   * filesystem-unmount-others — unmounting a drive somebody ELSE mounted.
//     Unmounting a drive YOU mounted never needs a password in the first place,
//     and the agent mounts as you, so the dock's unmount is already free.
//   * anything about formatting, partitioning, or changing a drive. Mounting is
//     reading and writing files; reshaping a disk still asks.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// CHECK IT DID WHAT IT SAYS
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The build (build_files/76-automount.sh) and CI both read this file to prove
// it still names subject.active and subject.local, returns YES for
// filesystem-mount AND filesystem-mount-system, and says NOTHING about
// encrypted-unlock, the -other-seat action, or any modify/format action — so a
// future edit cannot quietly turn it into "do anything to any disk with no
// password".
// ===========================================================================

polkit.addRule(function(action, subject) {
    // Only the person sitting at this machine, in the session that owns the
    // screen right now. Anything else is left to polkit's defaults.
    if (!(subject.local && subject.active)) {
        return polkit.Result.NOT_HANDLED;
    }

    // Mounting a drive (plugged in, or inside the computer), ejecting it, and
    // switching it off before it is unplugged. These, and ONLY these.
    if (action.id == "org.freedesktop.udisks2.filesystem-mount" ||
        action.id == "org.freedesktop.udisks2.filesystem-mount-system" ||
        action.id == "org.freedesktop.udisks2.eject-media" ||
        action.id == "org.freedesktop.udisks2.power-off-drive") {
        return polkit.Result.YES;
    }

    // Everything else: not our business, use the system default.
    return polkit.Result.NOT_HANDLED;
});
